---
import Base from '@layouts/Base.astro';
---
<Base
  title="What Toccata changed — KasStacker"
  description="The 2026 Kaspa upgrade in plain language: covenants, chain-enforced tokens, and zero-knowledge verification — what each actually means."
>
  <div class="wrap-narrow prose">
    <header class="head">
      <p class="eyebrow">Learn · stop 2 of 5</p>
      <h1>What Toccata changed</h1>
    </header>

    <p>
      <strong>Toccata is the 2026 upgrade that taught Kaspa to enforce rules on money, not just move it.</strong>
      Before it, the chain could answer one question: "does this signature match?" After it, the chain can
      check a whole contract's worth of conditions before letting coins move.
    </p>

    <div class="ba">
      <div class="col before">
        <h3>Before</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Send and receive coins</li>
          <li>Multi-signature wallets</li>
          <li>Token balances kept by <em>off-chain</em> bookkeepers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="col after">
        <h3>After</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Covenants</strong> — rules attached to coins that every future spend must satisfy</li>
          <li><strong>Chain-enforced tokens</strong> — the KCC-20 approach: supply and transfer rules checked by consensus itself</li>
          <li><strong>Inline zero-knowledge verification</strong> — the chain can check a cryptographic proof that something happened, without seeing the details</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <h2>The new words, translated</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Covenant</strong> — a lock on coins that dictates how they're allowed to move <em>next</em>. Not advice, not a bookkeeper's note: consensus rejects any spend that breaks it.</li>
      <li><strong>Lineage (KIP-17)</strong> — a covenant can prove where its coins came from, so rules can follow coins across many transactions.</li>
      <li><strong>Covenant ID (KIP-20)</strong> — a stable identity for a covenant, so one contract can own things and be found again.</li>
      <li><strong>Inline ZK (KIP-16)</strong> — zero-knowledge proof checking, live on mainnet since 2026-06-30.</li>
    </ul>

    <blockquote>
      Before Toccata, Kaspa was a very fast cash register. After Toccata, it's a cash register that can
      also hold escrow, run a vault, and issue tokens — with the rules built into the machine.
    </blockquote>

    <div class="next-link"><a class="btn btn-primary" href="/learn/meet-the-stack">Next: Meet the stack →</a></div>
  </div>
</Base>

<style>
  .head { padding: 56px 0 16px; }
  .head h1 { font-size: 2rem; font-weight: 700; margin-top: 8px; }
  .ba { display: grid; grid-template-columns: 1fr 1fr; gap: 16px; margin: 24px 0; }
  @media (max-width: 640px) { .ba { grid-template-columns: 1fr; } }
  .col { background: var(--surface); border: 1px solid var(--border-soft); border-radius: 10px; padding: 18px 20px; }
  .col h3 {
    font-size: 0.78rem;
    letter-spacing: 0.1em;
    text-transform: uppercase;
    margin-bottom: 10px;
  }
  .before h3 { color: var(--faint); }
  .after h3 { color: var(--teal); }
  .col ul { margin: 0; padding-left: 18px; }
</style>
